  "query_preview": "..(This Evening Through Saturday)\nIssued at 1217 PM MDT Sat Oct 24 2026\n\nSfc analysis shows strong cold front has pushed to the east of \nthe forecast area with northwest winds steadily diminishing \nthrough this morning. Upper level analysis and water vapor show a \nclosed upper low over AZ with convection moving through northern \nNM. Meanwhile to the north of the Dakotas, an upper low spins over\ncentral SK. Expect clearing skies through this afternoon thanks \nto height rises and drier air moving into the area. Highs this \nafternoon will be more seasonable, ranging from the upper 40s to \nmid 50s. \n\nOur next round of precip will move into the area Sunday afternoon\nas the upper low over the Desert Southwest begins to slowly move \ninto the region. Area of modest QG forcing along with near \nsaturated moisture profiles from around H850 to H700 will support \nprecip in the form of rain beginning overnight tonight into early \nSunday morning across south central SD and moving northward \nthrough the day as the upper low drifts northeast. Rain will \ncontinue through the overnight hours Sunday with the Black Hills \ngetting cold enough for some snow to mix in. \n\nThe upper low continues its trek across the region through Monday\nwith chances for precip continuing through the day. As colder air\nassociated with the core of the upper low moves into the area, \nsnow may begin to mix in with the rain, especially over \nnorthwestern SD and in and around the Black Hills. We may see a \ntransition to all snow over the Black Hills and far northwestern \nSD late in the afternoon to evening. In terms of accumulations, \nthere's still a little uncertainty as it will depend on when the \ncolder air moves in at the mid levels and the exact track of the \nlow. Even so, any accumulations are expected to be minor with some\nareas over the northwestern SD plains and Black Hills potentially\nseeing a dusting up to an inch of slushy accumulation. Any \naccumulations should be gone by Tuesday as warmer air moves back \ninto the region.\n\nUpper level shortwave and attendant moisture starved front will \nmove in from the PNW later on Tuesday. Could see some light rain \nshowers across far northeastern WY into northwestern SD with this \nsystem with the only other impacts expected being breezy southwest\nwinds across the western SD plains and portions of northeastern \nWY late Tuesday afternoon. Otherwise, upper level west-\nsouthwesterly flow will keep temperatures seasonably mild after \nTuesday with dry weather expected through the late week.",
